+# Hamilton (4,3)-cycle generator for arXiv:2302.04845
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | permutation |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (a cyclic block ordering) |
+| Intuition | invariant: a shared modular coordinate orders the blocks |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed: Section 1's alternating-block definition and Section 2.2's auxiliary adjacency graph |
+
+## What the problem is and why it is checkable
+
+[Wang and You, *Minimum degree thresholds for Hamilton \((\ell,k-\ell)\)-cycles in \(k\)-uniform hypergraphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04845) define a cycle by an alternating partition
+`(L_0,R_0,...,L_{t-1},R_{t-1})`, with both `L_i ∪ R_i` and `R_i ∪ L_(i+1)` hyperedges.  This family fixes `k=7, ell=4`.  It hands the solver actual disjoint 4-sets and 3-sets covering all vertices, plus the exact transition bits defining every permitted `R_i ∪ L_j` hyperedge.  The answer is a cyclic permutation of the block IDs.  Verification is just a permutation check and `n` exact transition-bit lookups; it accepts every valid ordering, not only the planted one.
+
+This is not a convenience graph analogue.  The L/R vertex sets and their 7-element unions remain in the instance and in `verify`.  The transition table is the induced portion of the auxiliary adjacency representation the paper defines in Section 2.2.
+
+## Why Track B is the honest claim
+
+Theorem 1.2 is an extremal existence result for `k>=7`, `k/2<=ell<=k-1`, sufficiently large vertex count divisible by `k`, and minimum ell-degree above `delta(n,k,ell)`.  It does **not** prove distributional hardness.  Section 1 quotes worst-case NP-completeness for general uniform-hypergraph Hamilton paths, which is also insufficient for Track A.  In fact, the originally suggested “plant a cycle and add random edges” construction becomes easy near the paper's roughly one-half minimum-degree threshold.
+
+The generated restricted family has an efficient algorithm, and the module says so.  A bounded-modulus recognizer tries every modulus below 10,000, compares the three residues on every block, sorts a consistent coordinate, and validates the resulting cycle.  Its complexity is `O(B*n + n log n)` for `B=10000`; at the shipping preset it used 2,159,568 modular reductions and 2,160,085 counted operations in 2.47 seconds for the G5 instance, and solved 8/8 G6 instances.  The compact route is to notice that the three tags on a block share a residue, recover the modulus from within-block differences, and order one tag per block by that residue.  After that insight the shipping route is bounded by 154 exact reductions/checks—within the no-tool cap, but very different from blindly executing two million reductions.
+
+The proof's easy structure was also respected: Section 2 uses reservoirs, absorbers, regularity, matchings and connectors, while Section 3 exposes the parity obstacle `f(B)`.  Those are existence/construction ingredients, not an average-case hardness license.  Generation therefore uses inverse construction rather than pretending the theorem supplies a hard distribution.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=7`)
+
+The smallest preset has six blocks (42 vertices) and can be solved on paper by following the six short transition rows:
+
+```text
+Blocks:
+0: L=[12,21,28,37] R=[0,24,39] tags=[93,126,159]
+1: L=[4,16,23,25]  R=[5,14,36] tags=[101,123,156]
+2: L=[10,13,15,40] R=[1,27,33] tags=[92,103,125]
+3: L=[17,22,29,41] R=[3,31,32] tags=[88,121,143]
+4: L=[6,20,30,38]  R=[7,11,19] tags=[91,102,157]
+5: L=[9,18,26,34]  R=[2,8,35] tags=[89,100,155]
+
+Transitions:
+0: 010100
+1: 100010
+2: 100001
+3: 000011
+4: 001100
+5: 011000
+```
+
+One answer is `<answer>[3,5,1,4,2,0]</answer>`.  `verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Dropping its last block returns `(False, "answer must contain exactly 6 block identifiers")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | blocks `n` | hypergraph vertices | out-degree | tag bits | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 42 | 2 | 4 | hand-scale illustration |
+| easy | 72 | 504 | 8 | 48 | shipping preset |
+| medium | 84 | 588 | 7 | 80 | available escalation |
+| hard | 96 | 672 | 6 | 112 | available escalation |
+
+No named preset has been rejected by a local gate.  `escalate()` first increases tag arithmetic and removes decoy shifts at fixed answer length, then increases `n`; it reports `cap_bound` before the intended route exceeds the 300-operation cap.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 16/16 planted certificates verified; every answer JSON-round-tripped |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | fenced JSON inside realistic prose round-tripped exactly |
+| G4 | 0 valid among 200,000 structure-aware random permutations; declared space is `72!` |
+| G5 | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo exact count 18; reference 2,160,085 operations |
+| G6 | six attacks × eight seeds, 0 successes; reference algorithm 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled to 144 blocks / 1,008 vertices; planted certificate still verified |
+| G8 | 40 relabelling/composition checks and 40 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 207 characters, about 145 tokens, 72 atoms, 154 intended operations—all within caps |
+
+The G6 failures cover regular-degree/public-index ordering, smallest-successor greedy, 512 random restarts, magnitude/decimal tag ranking, local neighborhood-overlap extremes, and generic directed-Hamilton DFS capped at 5,000 nodes.  The DFS visited 41,475 nodes across the eight instances.  The successful Track B reference performed 17,276,544 modular reductions and 17,280,680 counted operations across the same eight instances.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| run | preset | solved / attempts | status |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare hardening | easy | not scored | OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` for every attempted vendor; the harness correctly refused to count errors as failures |
+| structural hint | easy | not run | blocked by the same external account limit |
+| placebo hint | easy | not run | blocked by the same external account limit |
+
+No oracle hardness conclusion is claimed from these errors.  Once the OpenRouter allowance is restored, rerun the commands below; update `G9_EVIDENCE` from the three transcripts and regenerate `selftest_report.json`.  The structural hint names only the common-residue invariant.  `hinted - placebo` is currently unmeasured, not zero evidence.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2302_04845 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+answer = parse_answer("<answer>[3,5,1,4,2,0]</answer>")
+assert verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful hardening/G9 run:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2302.04845 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This family is deliberately Track B: a sandbox with big-integer arithmetic solves it quickly, and revealing the exact modulus may make the structural arm much easier.  The 0/200,000 guess result estimates only uniform random permutations after the obvious “use every block once” constraint; it is not a proof of low solution density and says nothing about informed priors.  The transition graphs are regular cyclic lifts, so stronger graph-isomorphism, SAT/ILP, color-refinement, or spectral/circulant-recognition attacks may recover coordinates; they were not run.  `canonical_key` is exact for the tested block-order and global vertex relabellings by normalizing attached tag coordinates, but it is not a complete unlabeled digraph-isomorphism canonical form.  Most importantly, the four-vendor oracle and both G9 diagnostic arms remain unmeasured because the supplied OpenRouter account is over its total limit; this directory must not be submitted as hardened until those harness-owned runs succeed.
